-- Q3548: System Availability and Change Management [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Tripling the processing capacity of a single existing server","Complete protection against all possible failure scenarios","Backup capability covering only half of the active components","One extra component beyond the minimum, covering single failures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per the Uptime Institute Tier Classification and NIST SP 800-34, N+1 redundancy means operating one more component than the minimum number (N) required for normal operations. This provides automatic failover protection against any single component failure at reasonable cost.
Wrong (A): Tripling capacity describes vertical scaling of a single server, not the N+1 redundancy model that adds one spare component.
Wrong (B): N+1 protects against single-point failures only; it does not guarantee protection against multiple simultaneous failures.
Wrong (C): Covering only half the active components describes an incomplete redundancy approach, not the N+1 model which adds exactly one spare.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3548;

-- Q3554: System Availability and Change Management [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Provider B is more reliable because its 1-hour MTTR means faster recovery from any failure","Provider A is more reliable because its 2,160-hour MTBF means far fewer disruptions overall","Both providers offer identical reliability since MTBF and MTTR cancel each other out","Neither provider can be compared because MTBF applies only to hardware, not cloud services"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1 and ISO/IEC 27031, Mean Time Between Failures (MTBF) measures average operational time between failures. Provider A''s 2,160-hour MTBF means approximately 90 days between failures versus Provider B''s 30 days, indicating significantly fewer disruptions.
Wrong (A): While Provider B recovers faster per incident, MTTR alone does not determine overall reliability; fewer failures (higher MTBF) often matters more.
Wrong (C): MTBF and MTTR measure different aspects of availability and do not cancel out; both factor into availability calculations independently.
Wrong (D): MTBF applies to any measurable system including cloud services, not exclusively to physical hardware components.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3554;

-- Q3556: System Availability and Change Management [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["RTO should be set below MTD to provide a safety margin for unexpected delays","MTD and RTO should always be set to the same value since they measure recovery","RTO should exceed MTD to allow extra time for thorough post-recovery testing","MTD and RTO are different names for the same metric, making equal values redundant"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1 Section 3.4, Maximum Tolerable Downtime (MTD) represents the absolute maximum time a process can be unavailable. The RTO must be set below the MTD to provide a safety margin, ensuring recovery completes before the tolerance threshold is breached.
Wrong (B): Setting RTO equal to MTD eliminates the safety buffer and risks exceeding tolerance if recovery encounters unexpected complications or delays.
Wrong (C): Setting RTO higher than MTD would guarantee exceeding the maximum tolerable threshold, defeating the purpose of establishing an MTD.
Wrong (D): MTD and RTO are distinct metrics; MTD is the business tolerance limit while RTO is the technical recovery target set within that limit.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3556;

