-- Q3562: System Availability and Change Management [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Back-out plans are unnecessary for most production changes","An untested rollback mechanism may fail when actually needed","Production changes rarely fail, making back-out plans optional","Corrupt backups are acceptable if the original change succeeds"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per COBIT 2019 BAI06.01 (Manage Changes), back-out plans must be tested before production changes to validate that the rollback mechanism actually works when needed. An untested procedure creates a false sense of security.
Wrong (A): COBIT requires validated rollback procedures for all production changes because any change carries a risk of unexpected failure requiring reversal.
Wrong (C): Production changes can and do fail; assuming otherwise contradicts fundamental change management principles that require contingency planning.
Wrong (D): Corrupt backups are never acceptable because they render the rollback plan useless precisely when recovery is most critical.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3562;

-- Q3563: System Availability and Change Management [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Data compression to reduce overall storage requirements","RAID distributing data copies across multiple storage devices","Data deduplication to eliminate redundant file copies","File archiving to move older data to long-term storage"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1 Section 5.1.2, RAID distributes and replicates data across multiple storage devices to provide fault tolerance. Different RAID levels (1, 5, 6, 10) offer varying combinations of mirroring and parity, ensuring data remains accessible if one drive fails.
Wrong (A): Data compression reduces storage size but provides no redundancy or fault tolerance if a storage device fails.
Wrong (C): Data deduplication eliminates duplicate copies to save space, which actually reduces redundancy rather than increasing it.
Wrong (D): File archiving moves data to long-term storage for retention purposes but does not distribute copies across devices for availability.$EXPL$
