-- Q3573: System Availability and Change Management [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Zero data loss on failover since every transaction is replicated first","Faster query performance for end users on read operations","Reduced storage costs achieved through real-time data compression","Elimination of the need for user authentication at the database"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-34 Rev. 1 Section 5.2, synchronous mirroring ensures every committed transaction exists on both the primary and mirror databases before acknowledgment, achieving zero data loss (RPO = 0). If the primary fails, the mirror contains an identical copy.
Wrong (B): Synchronous mirroring actually adds write latency because transactions must be confirmed on both servers; it does not improve query speed.
Wrong (C): Mirroring duplicates data across servers, which increases storage requirements rather than reducing costs through compression.
Wrong (D): Database mirroring addresses availability and data protection, not authentication, which is managed by separate access control mechanisms.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3573;

-- Q3574: System Availability and Change Management [easy/L1]
