-- Q3588: Security and Control Frameworks [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["All internal network traffic is inherently trusted by default","Trust is implicit once a user passes the perimeter firewall","Every access request must be authenticated regardless of location","Trust is established once per session and maintained until logout"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per NIST SP 800-207, zero trust operates on "never trust, always verify," requiring every access request to be authenticated, authorized, and encrypted regardless of network location. This eliminates implicit trust zones and assumes breach by default.
Wrong (A): Trusting internal traffic by default is the perimeter-based model that zero trust explicitly rejects, since insiders and compromised hosts pose real threats.
Wrong (B): Implicit trust after passing a firewall describes traditional perimeter security, not zero trust, which verifies every request independently.
Wrong (D): Session-based trust contradicts zero trust principles, which require continuous verification rather than one-time authentication per session.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3588;

-- Q3593: Security and Control Frameworks [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Personnel Security only applies to high-impact systems in NIST","Personnel Security is an optional control family under NIST SP 800-53","Personnel Security controls are already covered by Access Control","PS controls address screening, termination, and access agreements for IT"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5, the Personnel Security (PS) family includes controls for screening (PS-3), termination (PS-4), transfer (PS-5), and access agreements (PS-6), all of which directly affect information system security. Moderate-impact systems require these controls.
Wrong (A): Personnel Security controls apply at all impact levels (low, moderate, high); they are not restricted to high-impact systems only.
Wrong (B): PS is a mandatory control family in NIST SP 800-53, not optional; moderate-impact baselines include specific PS controls that must be implemented.
Wrong (C): Access Control (AC) and Personnel Security (PS) are distinct families addressing different aspects; AC covers logical access while PS covers human vetting.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3593;

-- Q3594: Security and Control Frameworks [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Controls may be implemented but certification requires an accredited audit","Self-declaration is sufficient to claim ISO 27001 certification status","ISO 27001 does not require any form of third-party audit","The client should switch to NIST CSF, which has no certification process"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Clause 9.2 and 9.3, formal certification requires assessment by an accredited third-party certification body. An organization can implement controls and self-declare alignment, but only an independent external audit constitutes certification.
Wrong (B): Self-declaration demonstrates intent and internal conformance but does not satisfy the external validation requirement for formal ISO 27001 certification.
Wrong (C): ISO 27001 explicitly requires internal audits and management reviews, and certification demands a third-party audit by an accredited body.
Wrong (D): Switching frameworks does not resolve the certification gap; NIST CSF and ISO 27001 serve different purposes and are not interchangeable.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3594;

